 "title": "Divergence and Convergence in Trading: A Novice's Guide",
 "introduction": "In the world of trading, understanding the concepts of divergence and convergence is crucial for making informed decisions. These terms refer to the directional relationship between two trends, prices, or indicators.",
 "divergence": {
 "definition": "Divergence occurs when two trends move further apart from each other.",
 "example": "For instance, if the price of an asset is rising while its indicator is falling, this is considered positive divergence.",
 "importance": "Divergence can indicate that the current price trend may be weakening, and in some cases, may lead to the price changing direction.",
 "technical analysis": "Technical traders often use divergence as a signal to trade, particularly when it appears in conjunction with other indicators.",
 },
 "convergence": {
 "definition": "Convergence happens when two trends move towards each other.",
 "example": "For example, if the price of an asset is rising while its indicator is also rising, this is considered positive convergence.",
 "importance": "Convergence is typically seen as a sign of a stronger trend, as it suggests that the asset's price and the indicator are aligned and reinforcing each other.",
 "technical analysis": "While convergence is less commonly used as a standalone signal, it can be a valuable tool for confirming the strength of a trend and identifying potential trading opportunities.",
 },
